# Acknowledged CLI surface removals, one `.surface` line per entry.
#
# scripts/check-surface-compat.sh fails a release whose .surface dropped a
# command or flag that the previous release had. List the dropped line here in
# the same PR (e.g. `hey todo --legacy-flag`) to acknowledge the break; prune
# entries after the release that ships them.

# `hey recordings <calendar-id>` listed every kind of recording a calendar holds, grouped
# by type, which meant every reader filtered the group they wanted and `--count` and
# `--ids-only` had to flatten across types to answer at all. Each type now has the command
# it already deserved: `hey event list` (new, plus add/edit/delete), `hey todo list` and
# `hey journal list`, the last two grown the `--calendar`, `--starts-on` and `--ends-on`
# flags so nothing `recordings` could read is out of reach.
hey recordings
hey recordings --all
hey recordings --ends-on
hey recordings --limit
hey recordings --starts-on

# Every command is now a singular resource with a verb. The six plural names were the
# exceptions — two were groups that merely read as plural (`accounts`, `contacts`), two
# were bare listings with no verb at all (`calendars`, `drafts`), and two mixed the shapes
# (`attachments <thread-id>` plus an `attachments save` subcommand; `threads <id>`, which
# reads one thread despite the name). The replacements:
#
#   hey accounts            -> hey account
#   hey attachments <id>    -> hey attachment list <id>
#   hey attachments save    -> hey attachment save
#   hey calendars           -> hey calendar list
#   hey contacts            -> hey contact
#   hey drafts              -> hey draft list
#   hey threads <id>        -> hey thread read <id>
hey accounts
hey accounts list
hey accounts use
hey attachments
hey attachments --allow-partial
hey attachments save
hey attachments save --force
hey attachments save --output
hey calendars
hey contacts
hey contacts add
hey contacts add --account-user-id
hey contacts add --alias
hey contacts add --email
hey contacts add --name
hey contacts bundle
hey contacts hide
hey contacts list
hey contacts list --all
hey contacts list --page
hey contacts note
hey contacts note delete
hey contacts note set
hey contacts note set --note
hey contacts note show
hey contacts show
hey contacts show-again
hey contacts unbundle
hey contacts update
hey contacts update --alias
hey contacts update --email
hey contacts update --name
hey drafts
hey drafts --all
hey drafts --limit
hey threads
hey threads --allow-partial

# The six older plural names had already been demoted to hidden aliases when `box`,
# `label`, `collection`, `workflow`, `clip` and `snippet` grew their `list` and `view`
# verbs. A hidden alias is a name nothing shows and everything has to keep answering, and
# a second listing path is a second place for a flag to drift — `hey labels --all` and
# `hey label list --all` were one test away from meaning different things. They now read
# as the singular families do:
#
#   hey boxes       -> hey box list
#   hey clips       -> hey clip list
#   hey collections -> hey collection list
#   hey labels      -> hey label list
#   hey snippets    -> hey snippet list
#   hey workflows   -> hey workflow list
#
# `hey box <name|id>`, `hey label <id>`, `hey collection <id>` and `hey workflow <id>` are
# untouched: those are bare arguments on a family that still exists, not separate commands.
hey boxes
hey boxes --all
hey boxes --limit
hey clips
hey collections
hey collections --all
hey collections --limit
hey labels
hey labels --all
hey labels --limit
hey snippets
hey workflows
hey workflows --all
hey workflows --limit

# `hey completion <shell>` printed a script and left the reader to work out where their
# shell reads completions from — advice that named root-owned directories and did not
# work unprivileged. It is now `hey shell-completion generate <shell>`, beside
# `hey shell-completion install`, which writes the file where the shell already looks.
# The bare-argument-plus-subcommand shape was the same one `attachments <id>` had.
hey completion
